cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "IDDSI-Open: an open-source smartphone grader for the 10 mL / 10 s IDDSI Flow Test, and a negative result on photo classification for IDDSI texture levels"
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "TODO"
    given-names: "TODO"
    affiliation: "LinguaLeap Tech Limited"
  - name: "IDDSI-Open contributors"
repository-code: "https://github.com/rayc0/iddsi-open"
url: "https://github.com/rayc0/iddsi-open"
abstract: >-
  IDDSI-Open ships an open-source deterministic computer-vision grader for the
  10 mL / 10 second IDDSI syringe Flow Test, together with code and recorded
  evaluation results for a SigLIP-2 CORAL ordinal photo-classification head for
  IDDSI texture levels L3-L7. The photo-classification head failed its
  pre-registered gates and the model weights and harvested image dataset are
  withheld as a deliberate, stated decision. The headline result is negative:
  two large vision-language model judges agree on only 31.0% of the same images
  (n_pairs = 575; Cohen's kappa unweighted 0.1062, quadratic-weighted 0.2766),
  because middle IDDSI levels require absolute size and flow information a
  scale-free photograph does not carry. A later version showed that the gate
  outcome depends on which label set the models are scored against, so every
  performance figure is conditional on a yardstick the project built itself.
  This is a research preview for culinary education. It does not perform
  official IDDSI tests, assess swallowing, determine suitability for any person,
  or decide whether food is safe to consume.
keywords:
  - IDDSI
  - dysphagia
  - texture-modified food
  - flow test
  - ordinal classification
  - calibrated abstention
  - negative result
